---
import Film from '../../plate/Film.astro';
import { renderFormula } from '../../../lib/formulas/render';
import { CUES, TIMELINE } from './story';
import './style.css';

const labels = [
  { id: 'step-sign', tex: String.raw`\pm\lambda^k` },
  { id: 'chi', tex: String.raw`\chi=-\log\lambda` },
  { id: 'conditional', tex: String.raw`\min\{1,\ln2/\chi\}` },
  { id: 'golden', tex: String.raw`\lambda=1/\varphi=(\sqrt5-1)/2` },
  { id: 'dimension', tex: String.raw`\dim_H\mu=\min\{1,h_{\mathrm{RW}}/\chi\}` },
  { id: 'copy-plus', tex: String.raw`x\mapsto\lambda x+\lambda` },
  { id: 'copy-minus', tex: String.raw`x\mapsto\lambda x-\lambda` },
  { id: 'finite-tail', tex: String.raw`|S_\infty-S_{14}|\le\lambda^{15}/(1-\lambda)` },
] as const;
---

<Film
  tag="uruk-overlap-fill"
  cues={CUES}
  timeline={TIMELINE}
  step={50}
  labels={labels}
  description="A carried histogram enumerates all sixteen thousand three hundred eighty-four sign choices in a fourteen-term Bernoulli sum while lambda moves from 0.4 to 0.9. An occupancy ruler counts filled bins without estimating dimension. Two affine copies come from the thirteen-term law. A conditional entropy-to-contraction guide is separate from the golden-ratio exact-overlap map counts and finite entropy trace. Blue marks the general claimed dimension formula. A dated review ledger distinguishes the catalogued statement from linked corollaries."
  caption="Every sign choice in a fourteen-term Bernoulli sum contributes to one finite histogram. Its occupied bins are not a density or a dimension. At the golden-ratio parameter, 1,048,576 twenty-letter words merge into 28,656 maps. The paper claims a limiting dimension formula using map entropy and contraction, with no separation condition."
  leftOut="Erdős's Pisot result; Solomyak's and Varjú's earlier results; absolute continuity; the proof's fair pairs; any numerical limiting dimension at the golden-ratio parameter; and an illustration of every signed, unequal-ratio system covered by the claim."
>
  <p class="film-method">How the film is made. The histogram enumerates all <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`2^{14}=16{,}384`, false)} /> equally likely sign words in <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`S_{14}=\sum_{k=1}^{14}\pm\lambda^k`, false)} />. It is the full fourteen-term finite law, not a Monte Carlo sample of the infinite sum. Repeated sums retain their multiplicities. The 64 equal bins cover <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`[-\lambda/(1-\lambda),\lambda/(1-\lambda)]`, false)} />, the infinite series' enclosing interval. For parameters at least one half the infinite attractor fills that interval; this does not assert a density for its measure. The omitted tail has absolute value at most <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`\lambda^{15}/(1-\lambda)`, false)} />.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The occupancy ruler records which of those 64 bins contain a finite sum. Occupied-bin share depends on the depth, bin width and enclosing interval. It is neither a density test nor a Hausdorff-dimension estimate. Every displayed finite law is atomic and has Hausdorff dimension zero. The limiting Cantor set below one half, and the limiting measure's dimension, are different objects. Thin finite tails near the larger parameters are not a newly opened gap in the infinite support.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The two self-similar pieces are the images of the thirteen-term law under <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`x\mapsto\lambda x-\lambda`, false)} /> and <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`x\mapsto\lambda x+\lambda`, false)} />, with equal weight. Together they give the fourteen-term law exactly. Applying those maps to another fourteen-term law would give fifteen terms instead. The carried-copy drawing keeps that depth distinction.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The contraction curve is <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`\chi=-\log\lambda`, false)} />. The fair-coin entropy is <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`\ln2`, false)} /> per symbol. The hatched guide <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`\min\{1,\ln2/\chi\}`, false)} /> is conditional on the absence of all exact overlaps, not merely the absence of the golden-ratio overlap. Its shape does not measure the finite histogram. At the golden parameter the guide's missing point records that its no-overlap premise fails; it does not plot a numerical dimension drop.</p>
  <p class="film-method">At <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`\lambda=(\sqrt5-1)/2`, false)} />, the identity <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`\lambda^2=1-\lambda`, false)} /> lets compositions be compared exactly through integer coefficient pairs. At twenty symbols, 1,048,576 equally weighted words give 28,656 distinct maps. Different maps need not have the same multiplicity. Their entropy uses the actual map probabilities, not the logarithm of the number of distinct maps. The drawn finite values <span set:html={renderFormula(String.raw`H(G_n)/n`, false)} /> are upper bounds on the limiting entropy rate, not that limit and not a measured dimension. No numerical limiting dimension at this parameter is supplied.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The claim is broader than the equal-weight, positive equal-ratio toy. It concerns every finite nonempty indexed family of contracting similarities of the real line with nonzero ratios of absolute value below one and every strictly positive probability vector. Ratios may be negative and unequal, and exact overlaps and repeated generators are allowed. The formula gives Hausdorff dimension, not absolute continuity or a density.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The closing evidence follows the article's date, 20 October 2026. No mathematician had publicly reported reading or checking the proof. SelfSimilar.lean is catalogued; the linked SelfSimilarCorollaries.lean is not catalogued. Absolute continuity is outside both. The statement defines Hausdorff dimension and the entropy rate itself, which need comparison with the paper. A kernel check is evidence about a formal statement, not approval or a reading of the proof. Without JavaScript, the article and this transcript remain available.</p>
</Film>

<script>
  import './element';
</script>
